{
  "book": "EXO",
  "chapter": 14,
  "resources": {
    "costumbres": {
      "c-0550ea8ca38b3e0c": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-120",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The word rendered captains is, literally, third men. Usually each war-chariot carried two men: the charioteer, who was an important character, and the warrior. Sometimes, however, there was a third man, who had direction of the two others. The strength of Pharaoh’s chariot force…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-120",
          "sourceHash": "f4c45ec94185f4d22acd445c8b0e39f95eb041f9b956bc71448fc742257dee1d",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-a96aff3d9e0638e6": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-119",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "The Egyptian chariot was a framework of wood, nearly semicircular in front, having straight sides and open behind. The front was of wood, and the sides were strengthened and ornamented with leather and metal bindings. The floor was of rope network, to give a springy footing. The…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-119",
          "sourceHash": "e4072b3a258106326109aac88e870ed6835295f3f39a323c6ed535d621b28e10",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-af6eade8f58db2eb": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-121",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Before the captivity, the Hebrews divided the night into three watches. The first was from sunset to ten o’clock; the second from ten o’clock to two; the third from two o’clock to sunrise. The first was called the “beginning of the watches” (Lam. 2:19). The second was called the…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-121",
          "sourceHash": "ee3653a07c3deab1ea7f957e4ddbdd05d9c19ce341dc5a228c43fba460f01e99",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
    },
